\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Remark}
\title{A commutator of normalized mass matrices}
\date{2026-11-26}

\p{\citet{(2)–(5), p. 1039, and (7)–(9), p. 1040}{jarlskog1985commutator}
uses two normalized Hermitian mass matrices, denoted there by
#{M,M'}. Her Hermitian commutator variable #{C} is defined
by #{[M,M']=\ui C}. In her three-family calculation,
#{\det C=-2FF'J}, with #{F,F'} the products of normalized
mass differences in the stated order and #{J} the imaginary
part of a mixing-matrix quartet. Consequently
#{\det[M,M']=\ui^3\det C=2\ui FF'J}. The letter #{C} in this
comparison is her commutator variable. The finite even
grading in \ref{fdip-009X} is a different operator.}

\p{For the coupling products in \ref{fdip-0193} the
commutator #{K=AB-BA} itself is skew-adjoint, and the trace
identity uses the unnormalized #{A,B} on the supplied
multiplicity space. Its proof is the finite word expansion.
The nonzero real-pair determinant in \ref{fdip-0192} uses
dimension two, where the three-eigenvalue identity does not
apply.}
